;you should probably make your own input functions because scanf and gets are
unsafe and too flexible with very specific constraints
this is an example of an input function that doesn't skip white space and ends
at a new line character:
int read_line(char str[], int n)
{
int ch, i = 0;
while ((ch = getchar()) != '\n')
if (i < n)
str[i++] = ch;
str[i] = '\0';
 /* terminates string */
return i;
 /* number of characters stored */
}

;some functions of the standard string library <string.h> in C:
1- strcpy:
char *strcpy(char *s1, const char *s2);
it copies s2 into s1, precisely the string pointed at by s2 to the array pointed at by s1
it returns s1, the new string
strcpy is unsafe in that it can't check if s2 can fit into s1 or not
so if s1 is n elements and s2 has more than n-1 characters undefined behaviour occurs

2- strncpy:
strncpy(str1, str2, sizeof(str1));
is a safer version of strcpy as it allows you to limit the number of elements copied

3- strlen:
size_t strlen(const char *s);
is a function that returns the number of characters of a string (without the null)
it includes white space
size_t is an unsigned integer

4- strcat:
char *strcat(char *s1, const char *s2);
strcat appends the contents of string s2 to the end of string s1
it returns s1
like strcpy, it's unsafe as it can't know if s2 can fit into s1 or noh

5- strncat:
strncat(str1, str2, sizeof(str1) - strlen(str1) - 1);
is a safer version of strcat that can be given the number of characters to be catted

6- strcmp:
int strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2);
it returns a number greater than, less than, or equal to 0
the number is less than 0 if the first i characters are the same but then
they're different in the (i+1) character, those two characters are compared based on
the character set, in ASCII smol>capital>number>space and z>a and numbers are 0 to 9 where 9>0
it compares s1 to s2 so if s1 is capital and s2 is smol the return is negative
it's also less than zero if s1 is shorter than s2
returns 0 if completely equal

